```java
/**
 * Pushes new Accounts to the ERP as customers.
 *
 *   Endpoint : Named Credential "ERP"  ->  callout:ERP/api/customers
 *   Method   : POST, Content-Type: application/json
 *   Body     : JSON array with exactly one customer per request
 *              [{"salesforceId":"001...","name":"Acme GmbH","country":"Germany"}]
 *   Response : 202 Accepted
 *
 * One HTTP request is sent per Account so that the ERP team can trace every Account
 * on its own. (The ERP API would accept up to 200 customers in one array, but batching
 * them would defeat that traceability requirement.)
 *
 * Why asynchronous: Apex forbids callouts from a trigger, so AccountTrigger only
 * enqueues this Queueable. The class is bulk safe as well: a transaction may perform at
 * most 100 callouts, therefore the Accounts are split into chunks of 100 and one job is
 * enqueued per chunk. A job that still has work left chains itself, so nothing is lost
 * even if more than 5,000 Accounts arrive at once.
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable {

    /** Named Credential endpoint. URL and authentication live in the Named Credential. */
    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';

    /** Content type required by the ERP API. */
    private static final String CONTENT_TYPE = 'application/json';

    /** The ERP answers 202 Accepted for a successfully queued customer. */
    private static final Integer STATUS_ACCEPTED = 202;

    /** Platform limit: queueable jobs that may be added to the queue per transaction. */
    private static final Integer MAX_JOBS_PER_TRANSACTION = 50;

    /** Customers this job is responsible for. */
    private final List<ErpCustomer> customers;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<ErpCustomer> customers) {
        this.customers = customers;
    }

    /**
     * Entry point called from AccountTrigger (after insert).
     * Splits the new Accounts into chunks of at most 100 (the callout limit) and
     * enqueues one job per chunk - one request per Account, never a callout from the
     * trigger itself.
     */
    public static void syncCustomers(List<Account> accounts) {
        if (accounts == null || accounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<ErpCustomer> customers = new List<ErpCustomer>();
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            customers.add(new ErpCustomer(acc));
        }

        List<List<ErpCustomer>> chunks = new List<List<ErpCustomer>>();
        Integer callsPerJob = Math.max(1, Limits.getLimitCallouts());
        List<ErpCustomer> current = new List<ErpCustomer>();
        for (ErpCustomer customer : customers) {
            current.add(customer);
            if (current.size() == callsPerJob) {
                chunks.add(current);
                current = new List<ErpCustomer>();
            }
        }
        if (!current.isEmpty()) {
            chunks.add(current);
        }

        // Never exceed the number of queueable jobs allowed per transaction; the last
        // job takes over the rest and chains the remainder from its execute() method.
        while (chunks.size() > MAX_JOBS_PER_TRANSACTION) {
            List<ErpCustomer> overflow = chunks.remove(chunks.size() - 1);
            chunks[chunks.size() - 1].addAll(overflow);
        }

        for (List<ErpCustomer> chunk : chunks) {
            System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(chunk));
        }
    }

    /**
     * Sends one request per customer and chains the leftovers, if any.
     */
    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        List<ErpCustomer> pending = new List<ErpCustomer>();
        Integer remaining = Limits.getLimitCallouts() - Limits.getCallouts();

        for (ErpCustomer customer : customers) {
            if (remaining > 0) {
                sendCustomer(customer);
                remaining--;
            } else {
                pending.add(customer);
            }
        }

        if (!pending.isEmpty()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(pending));
        }
    }

    /**
     * One POST with a one element JSON array for a single Account.
     */
    private static void sendCustomer(ErpCustomer customer) {
        HttpRequest request = new HttpRequest();
        request.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        request.setMethod('POST');
        request.setHeader('Content-Type', CONTENT_TYPE);
        request.setBody(JSON.serialize(new List<ErpCustomer>{ customer }, false));

        HttpResponse response = new Http().send(request);

        // Error handling and retries are explicitly out of scope for this ticket, so a
        // non-202 answer is only logged. There is no synchronous user waiting for this
        // job, so throwing here would neither roll back the Account nor help anyone.
        if (response.getStatusCode() != STATUS_ACCEPTED) {
            System.debug(
                LoggingLevel.WARN,
                'ERP rejected account ' + customer.salesforceId + ': HTTP ' +
                response.getStatusCode() + ' ' + response.getStatus()
            );
        }
    }

    /**
     * Payload for a single customer. JSON.serialize(..., false) keeps null values, so a
     * blank BillingCountry is sent as "country": null.
     */
    public class ErpCustomer {
        public String salesforceId; // Account.Id is always the 18 character Id in Apex
        public String name;
        public String country;

        public ErpCustomer(Account acc) {
            this.salesforceId = acc.Id;
            this.name = acc.Name;
            this.country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? null : acc.BillingCountry;
        }
    }
}
```

## Deviations

1. **The callout is not made synchronously inside the trigger.** Apex raises `CalloutException: Callout from triggers are currently not supported`, which is exactly what broke the previous version. The trigger still initiates the push, and the contract is unchanged: **one POST per Account** with a one-element JSON array, so the ERP team can trace each Account individually.
2. **The 200-record marketplace batch is split into two jobs.** One transaction allows at most 100 callouts, so 200 Accounts can never be pushed in a single transaction. `syncCustomers` chunks the Accounts by 100 and enqueues one job per chunk (never more than 50 jobs); a job that still has leftovers chains itself. The result is 200 requests for 200 Accounts, just spread over two (or more) async transactions.
3. **A non-202 response is only logged, not thrown.** Error handling and retries are out of scope per the ticket, and in an async job a thrown exception would neither roll back the Account nor be visible to the user. Also note the classic caveat of this design: the callout now happens in a separate transaction, so the ERP can be updated even if the Salesforce transaction is later rolled back — a reconcilable window that is inherent to pushing records on `after insert`.

